menu "My UPS Pal updates"

config UPSA_OTA_REPO
    string "GitHub repository to check for releases (owner/name)"
    default "aronreid/ups-to-esp32"
    help
        Where the board looks for firmware. The default is upstream's own
        repository, which is right for upstream's boards and wrong for a
        fork: assets are named ups-adaptor-<BOARD_OTA_TARGET>.bin, so a board
        with its own target finds nothing there and checks for ever in vain.

        A fork shipping its own boards points this at its own repository and
        publishes assets for its own target.

config UPSA_OTA_NO_AUTO_CHECK
    bool "Never check for updates unless the owner turns it on"
    default n
    help
        Upstream defaults the periodic CHECK to ON (ota.c), on the reasoning
        that a device which never looks can never tell its owner an update
        exists. Note that ota.h's comment says the opposite; the code is what
        runs.

        Turn this on for boards installed on other people's networks. A
        device reaching the internet on a timer from inside a customer's
        building is their decision, and the honest default for gear we
        install there is not to, until someone asks for it. The owner can
        still enable checking on the page, and installing always needs a
        person either way.

endmenu
